    crm:P183i_has_representation <https://ids.lib.harvard.edu/ids/iiif/493312443/full/full/0/default.jpg> ;
    rdf:value """In the evening I went out alone, B.B. being tired, to Mrs. Warder’s  Musicale , a great crowd of extravagantly dressed ladies and men in uniform, who barely sat through the music, bursting out into screaming talk before the las chords were struck. The Miss Warders are Italophiles, and seemed nice. I saw Mrs. Kuhn, the Miss Stickneys, Mrs. Slater (William Morris Hunt’s daughter), but met no one new - the crown was too great. A vulgar, hoydenish, very self-conscious girl was romping around, winking and beckoning all over the room, wriggling and giggling through the music. She turned out to be Miss Alice Roosevelt. 
Friday Feb. 12. 04 .
Went with Ellen Hale to the Smithsonian and saw the Secretary Mr. Langley, who has spent his life on an unworkable Flying Machine. Mr. Adler (Librarian) and Mr. Holmes (Ethnographer) took us round. Bernhard lunched with Secretary Hay, """^^xsd:string ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:index 173 ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:part_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/resource/diary/1903-04> .

